{"availability":"available","full":"sumanneogi/Car_Price_Prediction","name":"Car_Price_Prediction","description":"Explore this public GitHub project.","category":"Other","language":"Jupyter Notebook","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-06-04T14:15:09Z","updated":"2026-10-10T16:16:39Z","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":"https://car-price-analyzer.streamlit.app/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T19:02:57.070Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T19:02:57.070Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T19:02:57.070Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-10T19:02:57.070Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A used car price prediction system using machine learning that estimates a car's price based on features such as brand, model, mileage, engine volume, fuel type, registration status, body type, and manufacturing year. The project uses Linear Regression and Random Forest Regressor to learn patterns from car data and predict the estimated selling price based on the features provided by the user.","The main objective of this project is to understand the end-to-end workflow of a machine learning regression problem, including data cleaning, handle missing values and outliers, perform exploratory data analysis (EDA), feature engineering, model training, hyperparameter tuning(GridSearchCV), and model evaluation(MSE). The trained Random Forest model is integrated into a Streamlit web application, allowing users to enter car details and receive an estimated used car price.","Note: This project focuses on a supervised machine learning regression problem. Linear Regression and Random Forest Regressor are trained and evaluated to compare their performance, and the saved Random Forest model is used by the web application to generate price predictions."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/sumanneogi/Car_Price_Prediction/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"1f9521cb8d46e687bdc71abe7d13aac536b8606e05